=== REASONING ===

Migration strategy per dashboard:

TIRE PRESSURE (6 panels):
- Panels 1-5: Straightforward numeric signal pivot from signal_log.
  For timeseries (panel 1): GROUP BY ts with MAX(value_num) FILTER pattern.
  For stat (panels 2-5): ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1 with single signal.
  convert_pressure() function is a Grafana SQL function already defined — keep using it.
- Panel 6 (TPMS Warnings): Uses composite TypeTireLocation signals (TpmsHardWarnings,
  TpmsSoftWarnings) stored as JSON in value_text, plus last_seen_time signals stored
  in value_text as timestamps. Use last(value_text, ts) for text signals per rules.

ENERGY FLOW (8 panels):
- Panels 1-6: Numeric pivots from signal_log using MAX(value_num) FILTER pattern.
  Panel 1 (stat): Uses AVG aggregation — keep AVG via subquery or FILTER approach.
  Panels 2-6 (timeseries): Standard GROUP BY ts pivots.
- Panel 7 (BMS & System): Mix of text/bool/float. Use last(value_text, ts) for BMSState,
  last(value_bool, ts) for BmsFullchargecomplete/BatteryHeaterOn, and
  last(value_num, ts) for IsolationResistance.
- Panel 8 (Powershare): Mix of text/float. Use last(value_text, ts) for enums,
  last(value_num, ts) for floats.

PROJECTED RANGE (3 panels):
- All timeseries, straightforward numeric pivot.
- Panel 3 uses $__timeGroup which becomes time_bucket for TimescaleDB.
  The filter `battery_level > 10` needs to be applied via HAVING on the pivoted value.

Performance: All queries include ts BETWEEN $__timeFrom() AND $__timeTo() + signal_name IN (...).

